# mantleplace-dcc — line endings and binary marking.
#
# THERE ARE NO GIT LFS PATTERNS HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. A stranger's first clone of an
# open-source plugin must not be a multi-hundred-megabyte pull. The binaries in this tree — one
# .uasset file, three fonts, three PNG icons — total about 1.2 MB and are plain git blobs.
#
# If a real need for LFS ever arises (a binary submission that genuinely belongs here), that is a
# decision to take deliberately and once. Adding a binary type by accident is not reversible without
# rewriting history: git decides text-vs-binary at `git add` time, and LFS bills every version ever
# pushed, not just the current checkout. Ask before committing the first file of a new binary type.

* text=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary, so `* text=auto` never guesses and never rewrites their bytes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.uasset  binary
*.umap    binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.png     binary
*.ico     binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text that must keep specific endings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.uplugin   text eol=lf

# Ships inside the Revit release zip and gets opened in Notepad by someone who did not choose an
# editor. Not *.txt — the two font licence texts under unreal/ keep the bytes their authors wrote.
revit/packaging/README.txt text eol=crlf

# Hooks run under sh on every platform, Git for Windows included; a CRLF checkout breaks them
# with an error that names no file. LF always.
.githooks/* text eol=lf
